{#
  Choose one or several rows out of a list too long to show — type, wait a
  beat, ask the server, click a result (ARCHITECTURE.md §8.30bis).

  Four copies of that gesture existed before this partial: the camps « quel
  séjour ? » and the finance « quelle créance ? » and « quel mouvement ? »
  pickers, and the rental managers' search. Each one's comments said it was
  the shape of another. This is the shape, written once, in single AND
  multiple choice — the copies cover the first, the carpool event picker
  needs the second.

  **The select element stays in the page, and that is the part not to
  lose.** It is what the form submits when JavaScript does not run: a real
  list, holding the caller's shortlist and whatever is already retained,
  named exactly like the values the script will post. The script REMOVES it
  when it takes over, so the two controls can never both post the field —
  in single mode the one the browser sent first is not the one the reader
  chose, and in multiple mode a removed row would come back through the
  other control.

  (The tag is spelled out in words in this comment on purpose: to anything
  that lexes this file as HTML, angle brackets here are a real, label-less
  form control — SonarQube once failed the reliability gate on exactly
  that, see modules/camps/views/partials/stay_picker.html.twig.)

  The server side of the contract is Core\View\SearchPickerResult: the
  endpoint at `search_url` receives `q` and answers
  `{success: true, results: [{id, label, subtitle?, badge?, warning?}]}`.

  Usage:
  {% include 'partials/search_picker.html.twig' with {
      picker_id: 'carpool-events',       -- unique, prefixes every id inside
      search_url: '/covoiturage/…',      -- receives ?q= (or &q=)
      field_name: 'event_ids',           -- multiple mode posts event_ids[]
      label: 'Évènements concernés',
      mode: 'multiple',                  -- single (default) | multiple
      options: [ {value: 3, label: '…'} ], -- the list shown without JavaScript
      selected: [ {id: 3, label: '…'} ], -- already retained; optional
      placeholder: 'Chercher…',          -- optional
      empty_label: 'Aucun résultat.',    -- optional, said when nothing matches
      none_label: 'Aucun',               -- optional, single mode's empty choice
      help: '…',                         -- optional help text, ARIA-wired
      required: false,                   -- optional, single mode only
  } only %}

  The caller loads the script itself, once per page:
      <script src="{{ asset('/assets/js/search-picker.js') }}"></script>

  The script dispatches `search-picker:change` on the picker element, with
  `detail.selected` the retained rows ({id, label, …}), after every choice
  and every removal — for a page that reacts to what was chosen.
#}
{% set _mode = mode|default('single') == 'multiple' ? 'multiple' : 'single' %}
{% set _multiple = _mode == 'multiple' %}
{% set _selected = selected|default([]) %}
{% set _selected_ids = _selected|map(row => row.id ~ '') %}
{% set _submitted_name = _multiple ? field_name ~ '[]' : field_name %}
{% set _help_id = help|default('') ? picker_id ~ '-help' : '' %}

{# The fallback list: the caller's options, plus any retained row the
   options do not carry — without it, a retained value would silently drop
   out of the form the first time it is saved without JavaScript. #}
{% set _option_ids = options|default([])|map(o => o.value ~ '') %}
{% set _fallback = options|default([]) %}
{% for row in _selected %}
    {% if (row.id ~ '') not in _option_ids %}
        {% set _fallback = _fallback|merge([{value: row.id, label: row.label}]) %}
    {% endif %}
{% endfor %}

<div class="search-picker mb-3" id="{{ picker_id }}" data-search-picker
     data-mode="{{ _mode }}"
     data-search-url="{{ search_url }}"
     data-field-name="{{ _submitted_name }}"
     data-empty-label="{{ empty_label|default('Aucun résultat ne correspond.') }}"
     data-selected="{{ _selected|json_encode }}">
    <div data-search-picker-fallback>
        <label class="form-label" for="{{ picker_id }}-select">{{ label }}</label>
        <select class="form-select" id="{{ picker_id }}-select" name="{{ _submitted_name }}"
                {%- if _multiple %} multiple size="{{ min(max(_fallback|length, 3), 8) }}"{% endif %}
                {%- if required|default(false) and not _multiple %} required{% endif %}
                {%- if _help_id %} aria-describedby="{{ _help_id }}-fallback"{% endif %}>
            {% if not _multiple %}
                <option value="">{{ none_label|default('Aucun') }}</option>
            {% endif %}
            {% for option in _fallback %}
                <option value="{{ option.value }}"
                        {{- (option.value ~ '') in _selected_ids ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ option.label }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
        </select>
        {% if _help_id %}
            <div class="form-text" id="{{ _help_id }}-fallback">{{ help }}</div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {# Hidden until the script takes over: a search box that cannot search
       is worse than a list. #}
    <div class="d-none" data-search-picker-search>
        <label class="form-label" for="{{ picker_id }}-search">{{ label }}</label>
        {% if _multiple %}
            {# The retained rows, drawn by the script from data-selected so
               the markup and the posted values come from one place. #}
            <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-1 mb-2" data-search-picker-chosen></div>
        {% endif %}
        <div class="position-relative">
            <input type="text" class="form-control" id="{{ picker_id }}-search"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   {%- if placeholder|default('') %} placeholder="{{ placeholder }}"{% endif %}
                   {%- if _help_id %} aria-describedby="{{ _help_id }}"{% endif %}
                   aria-controls="{{ picker_id }}-results">
            <div class="search-picker__results list-group d-none" id="{{ picker_id }}-results"
                 data-search-picker-results aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
        {# What the form actually posts. Empty in the markup: the script
           writes one hidden input per retained row. #}
        <div data-search-picker-values></div>
        {% if _help_id %}
            <div class="form-text" id="{{ _help_id }}">{{ help }}</div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>
